 "notes_for_editor": "NRHP nomination (06000632) is not digitised. Sources disagree on 1883 incident: Lighthouse Friends says Keeper Singleton was killed by a train at Port Arthur and his wife and four children were found starving; NPS says Keeper 'W. Dermant' was iced out and the family (three children) wintered alone. Story states both. Heights disagree (LF 39.5 ft tower, focal plane 97.5 ft; USLHS 78 ft; record 23.8 m). NPS says manned to 1957; roster shows USCG crews 1960-67. Monarch/Hamringa rescue rests on Lighthouse Friends alone; 1906 newspapers read confirm only the wreck. The 1919 Bulletin p.1 passage on keepers removed by Canadians does not state the year of closure (index 1918-23 lists it at p.57 and ice-breaking tug at p.113). The 1933 Bulletin passage on the gale is in interleaved OCR columns; LF dates it 1932. Fog signal hours are from the 1893 and 1895 reports (scan pages matched, printed pages not verified). Gates tow rescue is from the Commissioner's report volume labelled 1921 (page unverified, scan 667). The 1920 Bulletin p.34 mentions tender Amaranth striking rocks at Passage Island on 23 July 1920 (OCR interleaved; not used). Roster dates for Hamringa second assistant 1905-1906 overlap Dec 1906 imprecisely. Fact-check 2026-10-27: Bulletin of 2 Jan 1919 p.1 is dated, so the closure was 15 Dec 1918. Gale report is Bulletin 3 Jan 1933 (storm Dec 1932). Monarch date disagrees (Argus 10 Dec 'yesterday' vs Lighthouse Friends 6 Dec). Commissioner item is in the FY1921 report (scan 667; printed page unverified). Roster completed from the USLHS page (125 lines, matched as printed); Coast Guard lines marked role other.",
